+# Release blocked: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match `SHA256SUMS`
+
+## What is blocked
+
+The release check cannot pass without either replacing the artifact with the
+correct bytes or changing the trusted manifest. Neither is possible from this
+repository.
+
+- Manifest entry (`SHA256SUMS:3`):
+  `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD`
+- Actual checksum of `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`:
+  `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce`
+
+The artifact on disk is therefore not the file the release was built and signed
+against. It is also not a readable gzip archive, though all three fixtures in
+`artifacts/` are opaque test data, so archive validity alone is not conclusive.
+The other two artifacts match their manifest entries.
+
+## Why it cannot be worked around here
+
+- There is no source, build recipe, or earlier revision in this repository from
+  which to rebuild `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`; git history contains only the initial
+  commit with the current bytes.
+- Updating `SHA256SUMS` to the checksum of the file on disk would make CI green
+  but would bless an unverified artifact. That defeats the purpose of the
+  release check and could ship a corrupted or tampered tarball.
+- Suppressing or excluding the failure in `verify.py` would hide a real
+  integrity mismatch and is not an acceptable fix.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two genuine bugs, now corrected:
+
+1. It read artifacts in text mode (`open(path)`), so any binary artifact raised
+   `UnicodeDecodeError`. It now reads in binary mode (`open(path, "rb")`).
+2. It compared a lowercase hex digest against manifest entries that may be
+   uppercase. The comparison is now case-insensitive (`expected.lower()`).
+
+As a result, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass. The release
+test `test_release_artifacts_verify` still fails solely because of the
+`libfoo` mismatch above.
+
+## How to unblock
+
+1. Re-fetch or rebuild `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from the trusted release source and
+   confirm its SHA-256 equals the value in `SHA256SUMS`.
+2. Or, if the artifact was legitimately rebuilt and the manifest is stale,
+   regenerate `SHA256SUMS` through the authorized release/signing process,
+   review and publish the new manifest, then re-run `python3 verify.py`.
+3. Once the artifact and manifest agree, the full test suite and the release
+   check will pass with the current `verify.py`.
